C o m p i l a t i o n


      CEE 6410 - Water Resources Systems Analysis
      Example Coups Minivans from Lecture Example 2 
       
      THE PROBLEM:
       
      A vehicle manufacturer can produce two different kinds of vehicles: Coups and Minivans.  Data are as follows:
       
       
                              Coups                       Minivans            Availability
      Metal               1x103 lb/vehicle           2x103 lb/vehicle        4x106 lb/year
      Circuit Boards      4 number/vehicle           3 number/vehicle        1.2x104 number
      Labor             5 worker days/vehicle     2.5 worker days/vehicle    17,500 worker days
      Profit/vehicle          $6,000                       $7,000
       
                      Determine the optimal manufacturing for the two vehicles.
       
      THE SOLUTION:
      Uses General Algebraic Modeling System to Solve this Linear Program
       
      David E Rosenberg
      david.rosenberg@usu.edu
      September 15, 2015
  25   
  26  * 1. DEFINE the SETS
  27  SETS vehicles Type of vehicle to produce /Coup, Minivan/
  28       res resources /Metal, CircuitBoards, Labor/;
  29   
  30  * 2. DEFINE input data
  31  PARAMETERS
  32     c(vehicles) Objective function coefficients ($ per vehicle)
  33           /Coup 6000,
  34          Minivan 7000 /
  35   
  36     b(res) Right hand constraint values (per resource)
  37            /Metal 4000000,
  38             CircuitBoards  12000,
  39             Labor  17500/;
  40   
  41  TABLE A(vehicles,res) Left hand side constraint coefficients
  42                   Metal    CircuitBoards   Labor
  43   Coup           1000      4                 5
  44   Minivan        2000      3                 2.5;
  45   
  46   
  47  * 3. DEFINE the variables
  48  VARIABLES X(vehicles) vehicles manufactured (Number)
  49            VPROFIT  total profit ($);
  50   
  51  * Non-negativity constraints
  52  POSITIVE VARIABLES X;
  53   
  54  * 4. COMBINE variables and data in equations
  55  EQUATIONS
  56     PROFIT Total profit ($) and objective function value
  57     RES_CONSTRAIN(res) Resource Constraints;
  58   
  59  PROFIT..                 VPROFIT =E= SUM(vehicles, c(vehicles)*X(vehicles));
  60  RES_CONSTRAIN(res) ..    SUM(vehicles, A(vehicles,res)*X(vehicles)) =L= b(res);
  61   
  62   
  63  * 5. DEFINE the MODEL from the EQUATIONS
  64  MODEL Vehicle_Manufacturing /PROFIT, RES_CONSTRAIN/;
  65  *Altnerative way to write (include all previously defined equations)
  66  *MODEL PLANTING /ALL/;
  67   
  68   
  69  * 6. SOLVE the MODEL
  70  * Solve the PLANTING model using a Linear Programming Solver (see File=>Options=>Solvers)
  71  *     to maximize VPROFIT
  72  SOLVE Vehicle_Manufacturing USING LP MAXIMIZING VPROFIT;
  73   
  74  * 6. CLick File menu => RUN (F9) or Solve icon and examine solution report in .LST file


